⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Working with ⟦P1⟧⟦P2⟧⟦P3⟧ (JavaScript Object Notation) is a simple data interchange format.⟦P4⟧Syntactically it resembles the objects and lists of JavaScript.⟦P5⟧It is commonly used for communication with networked ⟦P6⟧ services,⟦P7⟧but it is used in many other places, too.⟦P8⟧Its home page, ⟦P9⟧⟦P10⟧⟦PB1_11⟧,⟦P12⟧provides a wonderfully clear and concise definition of the standard.⟦P13⟧With the ⟦P14⟧encoding/json/v2 package⟦PB1_15⟧ it's a snap to read⟦P16⟧and write ⟦P17⟧ data from your Go programs.⟦P18⟧This package provides a cleaner ⟦P19⟧ and better defaults than the older⟦P20⟧⟦P21⟧⟦P22⟧⟦PB1_23⟧ package.⟦P24⟧Encoding⟦P25⟧To encode ⟦P26⟧ data we use the ⟦P27⟧⟦P28⟧⟦PB1_29⟧ function.⟦P30⟧Given the Go data structure, ⟦P31⟧,⟦P32⟧and an instance of ⟦P33⟧⟦P34⟧we can marshal a JSON-encoded version of m using ⟦P35⟧:⟦P36⟧If all is well, ⟦P37⟧ will be ⟦P38⟧ and ⟦P39⟧ will be a ⟦P40⟧ containing this ⟦P41⟧ data:⟦P42⟧Only data structures that can be represented as valid ⟦P43⟧ will be encoded:⟦P44⟧Pointers will be encoded as the values they point to (or ⟦P45⟧ if the pointer is ⟦P46⟧).⟦P47⟧⟦P48⟧ objects only support strings as keys.⟦P49⟧Thus, to encode a Go map, it must have a key that encodes as a ⟦P50⟧ string,⟦P51⟧such as ⟦P52⟧ (where ⟦P53⟧ is any Go type supported by the json⟦P54⟧package).⟦P55⟧Channel, complex, and function types cannot be encoded.⟦P56⟧Cyclic data structures are not supported.⟦P57⟧⟦P58⟧⟦P59⟧⟦PB1_60⟧ documents the full set of encoding semantics.⟦P61⟧The json package only accesses the exported fields of struct types (those⟦P62⟧that begin with an uppercase letter).⟦P63⟧Therefore only the exported fields of a struct will be present in the ⟦P64⟧ output.⟦P65⟧Decoding⟦P66⟧To decode ⟦P67⟧ data we use the ⟦P68⟧⟦P69⟧⟦PB1_70⟧ function.⟦P71⟧We must first create a place where the decoded data will be stored⟦P72⟧and call ⟦P73⟧, passing it a ⟦P74⟧ of ⟦P75⟧ data and a pointer to ⟦P76⟧⟦P77⟧If ⟦P78⟧ contains valid ⟦P79⟧ that fits in ⟦P80⟧,⟦P81⟧after the call ⟦P82⟧ will be ⟦P83⟧ and the data from ⟦P84⟧ will have been⟦P85⟧stored in the struct ⟦P86⟧,⟦P87⟧as if by an assignment like:⟦P88⟧How does ⟦P89⟧ identify the fields in which to store the decoded data?⟦P90⟧For a given ⟦P91⟧ key ⟦P92⟧,⟦P93⟧⟦P94⟧ will look through the destination struct's fields to find (in⟦P95⟧order of preference):⟦P96⟧An exported field with a tag of ⟦P97⟧ (see the ⟦P98⟧Go spec⟦PB1_99⟧⟦P100⟧for more on struct tags), or⟦P101⟧An exported field named ⟦P102⟧.⟦P103⟧What happens when the structure of the ⟦P104⟧ data doesn't exactly match the Go type?⟦P105⟧⟦P106⟧ will decode only the fields that it can find in the destination type.⟦P107⟧In this case, only the ⟦P108⟧ field of ⟦P109⟧ will be populated,⟦P110⟧and the ⟦P111⟧ field will be ignored.⟦P112⟧This behavior is particularly useful when you wish to pick only a few specific⟦P113⟧fields out of a large ⟦P114⟧ blob.⟦P115⟧It also means that any unexported fields in the destination struct will⟦P116⟧be unaffected by ⟦P117⟧.⟦P118⟧But what if you don't know the structure of your ⟦P119⟧ data beforehand?⟦P120⟧Generic ⟦P121⟧ with ⟦P122⟧⟦P123⟧The ⟦P124⟧ type describes any Go type.⟦P125⟧⟦P126⟧ is defined as ⟦P127⟧ (empty interface), which describes an interface with zero methods.⟦P128⟧Every Go type implements at least zero methods and therefore satisfies the empty interface.⟦P129⟧⟦P130⟧ serves as a general container type:⟦P131⟧A type assertion accesses the underlying concrete type:⟦P132⟧Or, if the underlying type is unknown, a type switch determines the type:⟦P133⟧⟦P134⟧ isn't one of the types above⟦P135⟧The json package uses ⟦P136⟧ and⟦P137⟧⟦P138⟧ values to store arbitrary ⟦P139⟧ objects and arrays;⟦P140⟧it will happily unmarshal any valid ⟦P141⟧ blob into a plain⟦P142⟧⟦P143⟧ value.  The default concrete Go types are:⟦P144⟧⟦P145⟧ for ⟦P146⟧ booleans,⟦P147⟧⟦P148⟧ for ⟦P149⟧ numbers,⟦P150⟧⟦P151⟧ for ⟦P152⟧ strings, and⟦P153⟧⟦P154⟧ for ⟦P155⟧ null.⟦P156⟧Decoding arbitrary data⟦P157⟧Consider this ⟦P158⟧ data, stored in the variabl