ot ⟦P221⟧.⟦P222⟧Streaming Marshal and Unmarshal⟦P223⟧The ⟦P224⟧⟦P225⟧⟦PB1_226⟧ and ⟦P227⟧⟦P228⟧⟦PB1_229⟧ interfaces are ubiquitous in Go, providing streaming access to resources such as ⟦P230⟧ connections, WebSockets, or files.⟦P231⟧The ⟦P232⟧ and ⟦P233⟧ functions allow marshaling and unmarshaling directly to and from these streams without requiring an intermediate ⟦P234⟧ holding the entire message.⟦P235⟧For example, we can write marshal output directly to standard out:⟦P236⟧Custom ⟦P237⟧ and Unmarshal⟦P238⟧Sometimes the default marshal behavior does not make sense for your type.⟦P239⟧For example, suppose we have a type describing a software version.⟦P240⟧This marshals as ⟦P241⟧, as expected based on the struct definition, but a version string like "1.2.3" would likely be a better representation for ⟦P242⟧ consumers.⟦P243⟧By implementing the ⟦P244⟧⟦P245⟧⟦PB1_246⟧ interface, we can provide a custom ⟦P247⟧ representation.⟦P248⟧⟦P249⟧⟦P250⟧⟦PB1_251⟧ provides unmarshaling of the custom ⟦P252⟧ representation.⟦P253⟧We can implement these method to convert to and from string representations.⟦P254⟧With custom marshaling, the ⟦P255⟧ value now marshals as ⟦P256⟧.⟦P257⟧Value must be a ⟦P258⟧.⟦P259⟧With custom unmarshaling, ⟦P260⟧ now unmarshals as ⟦P261⟧.⟦P262⟧This simple example requires the exact ⟦P263⟧ version format, but a more complex ⟦P264⟧ could choose to add flexibility, such as making the minor and patch versions optional.⟦P265⟧References⟦P266⟧For more information see the ⟦P267⟧encoding/json/v2⟦PB1_268⟧ and ⟦P269⟧encoding/json/jsontext⟦PB1_270⟧ package documentation.⟦P271⟧
